description:
User's request (2026-10-17): "put in a ticket for the scene launch tool to check the build state", after asking why a DSL-48 round took so long: much of an agent round is build/run/screenshot loops, and every scene launch rebuilds first.

Today: `ixdar-cli run-scene` and `ixdar-cli launch` (ixdar_automation_cli/cli_commands/run_scene.py _ensure_build, called from run_scene.py and launch_entry.py) run `mvn clean compile -pl annotations,ixdar-app` on every launch unless `--skip-build` is passed. The docstring gives the reasons for `clean`: an incremental compile reruns the annotation processors over only the changed sources, which prunes the generated registries and then dies in export-automation-routes; and both modules are built because the classpath file names the installed annotations jar, whose staleness shows up as a NoSuchMethodError. `--skip-build` instead uses whatever is on disk and only syncs resources newer than target/classes (sync_resources), so an agent must decide itself whether the classes are current, and a wrong guess runs stale code.

Wanted: the launcher decides. Before launching it compares the build outputs against their inputs and acts on the result:
- nothing changed since the last successful build (every .java, resource, pom.xml and annotations source older than the last build stamp, and target/classes present): no Maven at all, sync resources as today, launch;
- only resources changed: sync them, no compile;
- ixdar-app sources changed but no annotated type (registered nodes, routes, scenes; whatever the processors read) and not the annotations module: an incremental compile that keeps the generated registries, if that can be made correct; otherwise the full clean compile;
- annotations module or processor inputs changed, or the last build failed or was interrupted: the full clean compile as today.
The decision and its reason print on one line (e.g. `build: current (no source newer than 12:13:04)`, `build: clean compile (annotations changed)`), and `--skip-build` stays for forcing a stale launch. The state lives in a stamp file under target/ written only on a successful build, so an interrupted build is never taken as current. This must hold per worktree (each has its own target/).
